      "id": 1,
      "title": "Harbour notes",
      "content": "Morning light crept across the harbour wall while the fishing boats rocked at their moorings.\n\nNobody spoke. The tide was turning, and everyone knew what that meant for the day ahead.\n\nBy noon the nets were full and the arguments about who had caught the most had already begun.",
      "word_count": 49,
      "is_manual_book": false,
      "category_id": 1,
      "created_at": "2026-07-17T08:12:04.117Z",
      "updated_at": "2026-07-17T08:12:04.117Z"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "title": "The Winds of Kestrel",
      "content": "# Chapter 1\n\nThe tower at Kestrel Point had stood empty for eleven winters. Mara counted them on the walk up, one per switchback, and arrived at the door with cold hands and a stubborn kind of hope.\n\n# Chapter 2\n\nInside, the lamp room smelled of salt and old brass. She found the logbook where her grandmother said it would be, wedged behind the lens housing, its pages swollen but legible.\n\n# Chapter 3\n\nThe last entry was dated the night of the storm. It did not mention the ship. It mentioned only the wind, which the writer had called, in a careful hand, the winds of Kestrel.",
      "word_count": 109,
